cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "No fertility refund: speculative-decoding acceptance across 21 languages (data and code)"
message: "If you use this data or code, please cite it as below."
type: dataset
version: "1.2"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23348249
url: "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23348249"
authors:
  - given-names: Hanyu
    family-names: Yang
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-0419-4070"
abstract: >-
  Per-item speculative-decoding acceptance for two built-in self-drafters (Qwen3.5-9B MTP, Gemma 4 E4B
  assistant) on 200 FLORES-200 sentences in each of 21 languages, with the code that measures it. Under
  decision rules stated by the author before the runs (not independently verifiable; one gate amendment
  followed the first run), every gate passed and both kill rules fired in both families on their point
  estimates: acceptance does not rise with tokenizer fertility (it falls in Gemma and is flat in Qwen),
  and because every other language has lower acceptance length than English, the refund fraction is
  negative wherever it is defined. Qwen's half of the verdict rests on point estimates whose
  language-bootstrap intervals cross the thresholds. Run 2 predates recorded model revisions and code
  fingerprints; its files are exempted from those checks by identity, and the shipped runner and verdict
  code are later versions. A descriptive follow-up in vLLM 0.31.0 (wall-clock latency at batch 1, sampling at
  T = 0, 0.7 and 1.0, and two EAGLE-3 heads for Qwen3-8B) finds the same surcharge for both built-in
  drafters: every non-English language gets a smaller speedup than English, and acceptance does not rise
  with fertility under sampling. A multilingual EAGLE-3 head shows no trend over all 21 languages and is
  weak in English; excluding languages with more than 10% degenerate sampled outputs makes its trend weakly
  positive, with a sign that depends on the exclusion threshold (post hoc).
keywords:
  - speculative decoding
  - multi-token prediction
  - tokenizer fertility
  - multilingual inference
  - FLORES-200
  - negative result
  - vLLM
